</figure>
<div id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For the sphaleron signal, a limit on the cross section can be converted into a limit on the pre-exponential factor, defined in Section <a href="#Ch0.S3" title='0.3 Data and simulated samples ‣ A search for microscopic black holes, string balls, and sphalerons in proton-proton collisions at √𝑠=13⁢"ERROR \TeV"' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">0.3</span></a>. Following the method in Ref. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, we calculate the limits on the pre-exponential factor for five scanned <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{sph}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>sph</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{sph}}</annotation></semantics></math> values: 8.5, 8.75, 9, 9.25, 9.5<span id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\TeV</span>, under the assumptions of <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({N_{\text{CS}}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>CS</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({N_{\text{CS}}})=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. The observed and expected 95% <span id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\CL</span>upper limits on the pre-exponential factor as functions of <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{sph}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>sph</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{sph}}</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in Fig. <a href="#Ch0.F12" title='Figure 12 ‣ 0.9.2 Model-dependent results ‣ 0.9 Results ‣ A search for microscopic black holes, string balls, and sphalerons in proton-proton collisions at √𝑠=13⁢"ERROR \TeV"' class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.
The observed (expected) upper limit for the nominal <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\text{sph}}=9\TeV" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>sph</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="undefined">\TeV</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\text{sph}}=9\TeV</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ch0.S9.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({N_{\text{CS}}})=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>CS</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({N_{\text{CS}}})=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.0034 (0.0035), which is approximately 6.2 (3.4) times more stringent than the previous best limit of 0.021 (0.012) from CMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
